 they ride off to an  unknown future thomas noemi and their  children decide to stick with shea  joseph and his wife also want to go to  montana but shea refuses them saying  they are too sick to keep up noemi  demands they be allowed to join them  declaring to shay that they are now free  people whether they live or die she even  offers up her own services as driver of  their wagon as days drag on and joseph's  snake bite continues to worsen they're  forced to face the stark truth that he  might not make it to montana thomas then  offers yosef a choice they can amputate  the snake bitten leg or he can die with  it joseph chooses the only real option  and shea and thomas prepare to become  battlefield surgeons getting yosef drunk  enough to pass out they remove joseph's  leg in a brutal surgery by fire light to  which the rapidly dying elsa responds  sure glad i didn't get shot in the leg  before the surgery on yosef shea and  james sit around a fire and talk while  margaret sings elsa a lullaby james  explains that she only sings for their  children and that she paradoxically  promised to sing at his funeral despite  making him promise he'd die after her  however it's shay who tells the most  revealing story of the night he finally  opens up about his past and how his wife  helped him survive the civil war  according to shea his wife back home had  sent him a letter explaining that she  was leaving him for another man  prompting him to leave his unit and  return home with the intent to kill his  wife's new lover when he got there it  turned out to be a ruse designed to get  him to leave the war ultimately saving  him from death on the battlefield at  gettysburg and reuniting the couple it's  a touching story one that gives new  meaning to shae's longing for the family  we saw him leave behind in the premiere  episode the story illustrates the  sadness in shae's past and the passion  that drives him throughout the series it  also helps explain the respect he has  for james and his relationship with his  wife and daughter the love he sees in  their eyes proves that he's more than  just a hardened war veteran  the settler's decision to proceed to  oregon without shea was met with  derision from the cowboy who warned them  they would die if they attempted to go  it alone he insisted that their best  hope for survival was to spend the  winter in the valley and set off for  oregon in the spring but the remaining  group had had enough of brennan's delays  even with his protection he had gotten  many of them killed crossing a river  with more dying due to accidents and  attacks the settlers also felt that at  this point in the journey the trail was  well marked enough that they didn't need  shea as a guide a decision which would  quickly doom them  not long after splitting up a group of  bandits attacked the wagon train looting  it and killing everyone they found it  was another grim reminder of the  lawlessness and brutality of life on the  planes and the dangers that lurk around  every corner they came to american  shores with dreams of freedom and  prosperity but in the end they wound up  meeting their fate at the end of an  outlaws pistol  though all hope seems lost for elsa as  james shea and the remaining members of  the group take her north towards montana  to die they have an unexpected run-in  with a band of friendly native americans  the leader of the tribe an elder named  spotted eagle sees that elsa is in rough  shape and says he has a doctor in his  own group that may be able to help her  what do your  doctors say  you have no doctors  i'll get you a doctor  after days spent injured on the trail  elsa finally gets proper attention for  her wound from two medicine women who  take her to a nearby stream and flush  out the infection audiences may have  believed this was the moment when things  would change for elsa and she might  finally be saved it would have made  thematic sense if she were healed by the  tribe since she had bonded to a native  american man earlier in the season  unfortunately the tribe's prognosis  wasn't any better than the forts the  leader of the tribe explains that the  lakota coat the tips of their arrows in  cow manure to ensure that their victims  are killed by infection if not from the  wound itself despite their medical  attention and a spiritual ceremony it  seems that there was truly no saving the  young elsa  dutton having accepted his daughter's  fate james tells spotted eagle of his  intention to take elsa into the  mountains to die he explains that  wherever elsa lays is where he and his  wife plan to lay claim and build a ranch  he goes on to say he does not know this  region and fears it will not be  hospitable to him in response spotted  eagle suggests a valley he hunted while  growing up the winters are  cruel but the summers are rich in a man  who  plans can thrive  spotted eagle says it is called paradise  valley which yellowstone fans will  recognize as the real life valley where  the series is set it's also the name of  a capital investment firm run by dan  jenkins that tries to push the duttons  off the yellowstone more than a hundred  years later  spotted eagle also gives james a word of  warning telling him that in seven  generations his people will rise up to  reclaim their land the one thing he asks  of james is permission to continue  hunting in the valley which she happily  gives this also explains james kindness  towards a small tribe we saw him meet in  flashbacks in yellowstone as he owes  them a great debt for helping elsa and  pointing them to their new home in the  valley  finally realizing that she won't get  better and will soon die elsa feels  betrayed that her father lied to her  while speaking to her father she  requests to choose the spot where she is  to die james grants her request as  margaret watches from a distance  distraught that her daughter has to face  the truth of her own demise however it's  once again wisdom from shea brennan that  helps james come to terms with elsa's  impending doom the veteran soldier  consoles him with the fact that elsa  lived a full life fuller than most  despite her young age she had outlived  us all  elsa had a happy childhood and a loving  family she traveled the planes rode  horses joined a wagon train saw the  beauty of the land and bonded with many  of its inhabitants she clashed with  comanche and battled bandits  she fell in love more than once and was  loved back elsa had indeed lived the  american dream and death was now the  last destination on a short journey  through this world  in this is not your heaven fans of  yellowstone finally get the significant  connections they've been looking for all  season long we learn what pulled james  dutton off the path to oregon to  founding the yellowstone ranch after  years of hints and references to family  legacy we finally understand the spark  that ignited the dutton's passion for  their home in montana in an early part  of the episode else amuses to her father  that the only thing she fears about  death is being forgotten james and  margaret vowed that if she's to die they  will make their home where they bury her  so they can visit her grave every day  ensuring her memory when elsa chooses a  spot by a tree near where the dutton  ranch is to be built audiences finally  understand the dutton's ancestral claim  to the land we've heard john dutton's  promise to his father not to let the  land go and now we see that the same  promise has been handed down for  generations it seems that dutton's claim  to the yellowstone ranch stretches back  to 1883 with the death of elsa dutton  while her descendant john dutton refuses  to relinquish it it remains to be seen  if spotted eagle's prophecy that his  people will one day reclaim it is true  once elsa passes away in her father's  arms the story flashes forward one year  while we do not see james and margaret  in their new ranch we do get updates on  the surviving cast joseph is seen on  crutches his right leg amputated as he  removes his wedding band in memory of  his wife he pulls a stack of wood planks  from his horse and we're left to believe  that he is building his new home in a  valley he is the lone survivor of his  group but he has finally found the home  he set out to build when the series  began we then cut to willamette valley  oregon and see noemi and thomas staking  a claim on a plot of land near a river  naomi's two children rushed to embrace  her this is especially meaningful  because earlier we learned that her  children blamed her for the death of  their father and hadn't spoken to her  since finally in oregon they build a new  home and new life with thomas  finally we see shea on a beach looking  out over the water he speaks to his late  wife helen admiring the beauty of the  pacific ocean having completed his  journey and seeing nothing left to hang  on to  shea ends his own life while sitting on  the shoreline whether heart warming or  devastating the ending of 1883 won't  leave a dry eye in the house  when the series began audiences may have  suspected that 1883 would be a series  very much like yellowstone a high octane  drama full of action and suspense the  wild west seemed like the perfect place  for the series first spin-off and surely  fans dreamed of rough-riding cowboys  chasing down bandits in a fast-paced  western adventure when it premiered  viewers were instead treated to a somber  and emotional drama a slower more  intimate look at life on the frontier  than they may have expected while james  indeed played a central role it became  clear right away that it would be elsa  dutton's story as she narrated her  journey north from texas an unexpectedly  subdued premiere episode gave way to a  series that provided background to  yellowstone while telling its own story  that required no prior knowledge perhaps  the most surprising thing about 1883 is  that it wasn't the story of hope and  discovery that many may have thought  instead the series was ultimately a  bleak tale of the hopelessness and  sorrow felt by so many as they ventured  out onto the frontier though there are  moments of joy to be found even those  are bittersweet as so many of the series  characters meet untimely ends the  unforgiving narrative we're left with  illustrates how every element of early  american life was fraught with danger  and death  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

